## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a multi-protocol API platform that bridges REST, GraphQL, and gRPC while handling multi-tenancy, contract versioning, and consistent data mapping for common resources like invoices.

## Key Concepts

- OpenAPI-driven REST surface generation
- GraphQL schema stitching across microservices
- gRPC bridging/translation layer
- Multi-tenancy: tenant_id, per-tenant authentication
- Contract versioning via headers and OpenAPI tags
- Data mapping for resource boundaries (invoice)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: REST handler delegates to GraphQL or gRPC
async function handleInvoiceRequest(req, res) {
  const tenantId = extractTenantFromJWT(req.headers.authorization);
  const version = req.headers['api-version'] || 'v1';

  // Route to appropriate backend based on protocol
  if (req.path.startsWith('/graphql')) {
    return await graphqlResolver(tenantId, req.body.query, version);
  } else if (req.path.startsWith('/grpc')) {
    return await grpcBridge(tenantId, req.method, req.body, version);
  } else {
    return await restHandler(tenantId, req.path, req.body, version);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-protocol data governance: masking at the boundary across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC, driven by per-tenant OpenAPI contracts. It tests how to derive and enforce field visibility, roll out changes safely, and audit policy drift.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant field-level visibility across protocols
- Contract-driven masking rules derived from OpenAPI
- Central registry (Redis) for tenant/resource visibility
- Canary rollouts and rollout safety across services

## Code Example

```javascript
function maskVisible(obj, tenantId, contract) {
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(v => maskVisible(v, tenantId, contract));
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  const out = {};
  const allowed = contract?.visibleFields || [];
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(obj)) {
    out[k] = allowed.includes(k) ? v : 'REDACTED';
  }
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift and automated rollback when masking changes break clients?
- What metrics would you collect to detect masking-related regressions across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?

## Why This Is Asked
